    "media:imagetext": "Chapter 1.\nCONCERNING LOGS\nHEN Captain Adrian Perkins of the good ship\n\"Martha\" of New Bedford plied the Atlantic in\nsearch of whales-so that our forefathers might\nhave oil to light their evenings at home he kept a\nlog.\nA picturesque thing this seaman's log\nchock-full of adventure from the start to the journey's\nend. Written in the vacant hours at sea, between\nthe exciting moments of whale chasing and the strenuous hours-of\nbattling North Atlantic storms.\nIf vou tore a page from Cap'n\nPerkins' log of the year 1803, you'd find it looked like this:\nto Be tind of the shones harthe\nRemarks onboard ye\n• 15, 1815\n•інацисві\nabout\n18$\n• Purit. a\nEid 82\nMidde fort antines fluoant\n• dig hiti\nLeat. ..\n-from Cap'n\nPerkins' Los.\nThe whalers have gone now,-swept from the sea by the dis-\ncovery of oil. Today we have a new kind of log-the log of the\npathfinder. Peary in his dash for the North Pole, Scott in his fatal\nwanderings in the Antarctic Circle, Read in his hop to Europe via\nthe Azores, Alcock and Brown in their thrilling non-stop fight\nfrom Newfoundland to Ireland, Ross Smith in his flight from\nand there was Miss Liberty\nWaving Us a Welcome!\"",
